  "notes": "Conventions. (1) 'is commonly eaten without cooking' = there is a widespread standard preparation in which the eater consumes it with no cooking step; it does not require the food to be unprocessed. True for cheese, cured meats, bread, nuts, condiments, most fruit and salad vegetables, and for salmon (sashimi/gravlax/lox are mainstream). False for foods whose ordinary preparation is cooking even though a niche raw dish exists (beef -> tartare, tofu -> hiyayakko, broccoli -> crudites), and for garlic and ginger, which are predominantly cooked. (2) 'is fermented' = standard production includes a deliberate microbial fermentation step (cheddar, yogurt, sourdough, tempeh, miso, soy sauce, fish sauce, kimchi, brine-cured olives, vinegar, and chocolate, whose cacao beans are fermented). Salt/dry curing without fermentation is False, so prosciutto and salt-cured anchovies are False. (3) 'is high in fat' = roughly 10 g fat per 100 g as eaten. Whole milk (3.5%), plain yogurt, chicken breast, shrimp, water-packed tuna and tofu fall below. (4) 'is high in protein' = roughly 15 g per 100 g in the form purchased, or the food is a primary protein source in its cuisine (tofu at ~8 g qualifies on that clause; egg at ~13 g likewise). Quinoa-like borderline grains were dropped rather than guessed at. Nori and miso are protein-dense per 100 g but eaten in gram quantities, so False. (5) 'tastes noticeably sweet' = sweetness is a prominent taste as eaten, not merely 'not bitter'; carrot, beetroot, butternut squash, sweet potato and canned coconut milk qualify, tomato and milk do not. Dark chocolate is True (added sugar dominates over the cacao bitterness at typical 60-75% bars). (6) 'is substantially processed' = transformed from raw agricultural form by milling, splitting, extraction, pressing, refining, curing, or culturing. Rolled oats (hulled, steamed, flattened) and red lentils (hulled and split) are True; brown rice (hulled only) and dried chickpeas are False. Honey is False (filtered only). (7) 'is shelf-stable' = keeps months at ambient temperature without refrigeration; includes dry-cured prosciutto, canned goods, and cellar-keeping produce (potato, sweet potato, onion, garlic, winter squash) but not fresh cassava, bread, or tortillas. (8) 'is used mainly as a seasoning, condiment, or cooking fat' covers flavor-contributing ingredients rarely eaten as a dish on their own, which is why butter and olive oil are True and avocado is False; kimchi is True as a banchan/condiment. (9) 'is a common food allergen' = a member of the standard major-allergen list (milk, egg, fish, crustacean shellfish, tree nut, peanut, wheat, soy, sesame); coconut is True because US regulation classifies it as a tree nut; sunflower seed is False. (10) Honey is animal-derived (bee product). Mushrooms and seaweed are neither plant nor animal and are simply False on 'is of animal origin'. (11) 'grows underground' includes tubers, roots, bulbs and the geocarpic peanut. (12) Near-synonyms were pruned deliberately (walnut alongside almond, cucumber/kale alongside tomato, banana/mango alongside apple, quinoa alongside brown rice) because they produced identical rows; substitutes were chosen to keep the food space wide while keeping rows distinct.",
